# AUTHOR-PAREN PRESERVATION — the OWNERSHIP-SIGIL axis.
#
# `for i in (^start)..end:` is the shape where preservation and the formatter's
# OWN required wrap meet on two different nodes. The `^` is stripped by a token
# pre-pass into the enclosing node's `ownership` field, so an expression that
# legitimately STARTS with a sigil has to be parenthesised by
# `format_ownership_modifier_operand` or it round-trips into a DIFFERENT AST.
# The author's paren sits on `^start`; the required wrap belongs to the RANGE.
#
# RATIFIED output: `((^start)..end)` — the author's paren plus the formatter's
# required one, both on the page, idempotent. Suppressing the range's wrap
# because a DIFFERENT node happens to carry a paren is the one thing the design
# refuses: same-span suppression only.
#
# Both cells are TEXT-ONLY, deliberately: `for i in (^start)..end:` and
# `for i in (^start..end):` are REJECTED (E_MoveInOperandPosition) before and
# after this change — that is the point, the reject must survive the round
# trip. The pre-fix binary rewrote the first spelling to the second, a
# different AST that happens to reject for a different reason. Sigil cells that
# RUN live in `sigil_run.gg`.
#
#   * `(^start)..end` → `((^start)..end)`. RED on the pre-fix binary, which
#     prints `(^start..end)`.
#   * `(^start..end)` → unchanged. POS PIN: the sigil sits INSIDE the author's
#     paren, so the author's paren and the formatter's required wrap are the
#     SAME node and E1's same-span dedup emits exactly one pair. No red is
#     constructible against any variant tried; it is here because removing that
#     dedup reproduces accretion at exactly this position.

void iterate(int start, int end):
    for i in (^start)..end:
        print(i)

void iterate_inner(int start, int end):
    for i in (^start..end):
        print(i)
